@en "graphql"
@fa "گراف‌کیوال"
@ar "جراف‌کیوال"
package graphql

// A complete, dependency-free GraphQL implementation: lexer, parser for
// both executable documents and the Schema Definition Language, a schema
// model, a spec-shaped validator, an executor with correct error
// propagation, full introspection, and HTTP transport plus a client.
//
// Nothing here reaches outside the standard library. The dynamic value
// tree is encoding.value (the same one json/yaml/msgpack/rpc share), so
// a resolver result can be handed straight to any of those encoders, and
// a GraphQL server composes with net/http's router the same way
// std/rpc's dispatcher does.
//
// Layout, lowest layer first:
//
//   gql_token.salam     lexical token kinds
//   gql_lexer.salam     source text to tokens
//   gql_lexutil.salam   escapes, UTF-8, block-string dedent
//   gql_ast.salam       arena-backed AST for both document flavours
//   gql_parser.salam    executable documents (query/mutation/fragment)
//   gql_sdl.salam       Schema Definition Language
//   gql_schema.salam    runtime schema model and programmatic builder
//   gql_print.salam     AST and schema back to source text
//   gql_error.salam     spec-shaped errors with path and location
//   gql_coerce.salam    variable, input and result coercion
//   gql_resolver.salam  resolver registry and the slot-based handler ABI
//   gql_execute.salam   the execution algorithm
//   gql_validate.salam  validation rules run before execution
//   gql_introspect.salam  __schema, __type, __typename
//   gql_http.salam      GraphQL-over-HTTP request and response handling
//   gql_client.salam    query builder and response reader
//
// Why an arena AST. GraphQL syntax is deeply recursive (a selection set
// holds fields that hold selection sets), and a Salam struct that
// contained a Vector of itself would be copied by value at every level,
// with all the aliasing hazards encoding/value's header documents. The
// AST therefore stores nodes in flat Vectors on the Document and refers
// to children by integer index, so a subtree is passed around as one
// int. Nothing is deep-copied and nothing aliases.
//
// Why resolvers take an integer slot. Salam has no closures, and
// `callhandler(fn, arg)` invokes a `void(i64)` function pointer, so a
// resolver can neither capture its schema nor return a value directly.
// The executor parks the field's parent, arguments and context in a slot
// and passes that slot's index; the resolver reads them back through
// graphql.Arg* and writes its answer with graphql.Resolve*. This mirrors
// std/rpc's handler ABI exactly, for the same underlying reason.
//
// Known deviations from the specification, both consequences of
// building on the shared standard library rather than oversights:
//
//   Response key order. Spec 7.1.2 asks that response keys come back in
//   the order the query asked for them. `data` is an encoding.value
//   tree whose objects are HashMap-backed, so iteration is bucket order
//   rather than insertion order. Key order is not semantically
//   meaningful to any JSON reader, and ResultToJSONStable gives a
//   reproducible ordering when a stable byte sequence is wanted.
//
//   Subscriptions have no live transport. Subscription documents parse,
//   validate (including the single-root-field rule) and execute against
//   a value, but there is no WebSocket protocol implementation here.
//
// Everything else in sections 2 through 7 is implemented: the full
// grammar for both document flavours, input and result coercion, the
// execution algorithm with error propagation, the validation rules,
// and introspection.

// Package version, following the spec edition this implementation
// targets rather than the library's own release history.
pub const SpecEdition := "October2021"
